# =============================================================================
# .env.example - configuration template for the Excel Clone backend
# =============================================================================
# Sections A to C are the complete list of configuration keys the backend reads. Copy it
# to `.env` in the repository root and edit the copy:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Not every value below is a placeholder, and the difference matters - changing one of the
# second kind weakens a control rather than configuring it. Each key says which it is:
#
#   REPLACE  - a stand-in that names nothing real. Every key in Section A except ALGORITHM
#              and ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES, the two bucket and project names in
#              Section B, and every value in Sections C and E. The application will not
#              start, or will not reach the right project, until these are replaced.
#   KEEP     - the value config.py already defaults to, reproduced here so the surface is
#              visible. ALGORITHM, ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES, signed_url_expiry_minutes,
#              db_sslmode, auth_token_verifier, auth_enforcement_enabled, rate_limit_enabled,
#              rate_limit_default, rate_limit_write and csp_report_only. Four of these are
#              security switches whose default is the secure position; a comment on each says
#              what changing it costs.
#   REVIEW   - ALLOWED_ORIGINS. Its example is a working local-development value and a
#              deliberately narrow one, but a deployment needs its own origins added.
#
# The keys must reach the process environment. Any of these does that:
#
#     PYTHONPATH=. uvicorn --env-file .env backend.app.main:app
#     set -a; . ./.env; set +a                   # bash or zsh, see the caveat below
#     an env / envFrom entry on the Kubernetes pod spec
#
# Do NOT use `export $(grep -v '^#' .env | xargs)`. `xargs` strips the quoting that
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS needs and word-splits on the spaces inside a JSON array, so the value
# reaches Pydantic malformed and validation fails. `uvicorn --env-file` parses the file
# itself and is the reliable option. If you dot-source instead, single-quote every JSON
# value in `.env` - ALLOWED_ORIGINS='["http://localhost:3000"]' - because the shell
# otherwise removes the inner double quotes and the JSON no longer parses.
#
# Do NOT commit `.env`. It holds SECRET_KEY and the DATABASE_URL password. The root
# `.gitignore` covers `.env` and `.env.*` with an explicit `!.env.example` exception,
# so a populated `.env` no longer appears in `git status` - confirm with
# `git check-ignore -v .env`. That rule is the safety net, not a substitute for
# care: if a secret ever does reach history, deleting it in a later commit does not
# remove it, and the remedy is to rotate the credential.
#
# `Settings` in `backend/app/core/config.py` is the source of truth for the key
# names and types below; this file mirrors it. `Settings` uses Pydantic v1, which
# matches environment variable names case-insensitively, so an upper-case variant
# of a lower-case key below also resolves.
#
# ONE CANONICAL INPUT SET
# -----------------------
# Several keys below are the same fact as a Terraform variable and a frontend build
# variable. A deployment is coherent only when all three agree, and divergence does
# not fail loudly - it surfaces as refused sign-ins, CORS rejections, or a
# Content-Security-Policy that blocks every API call.
#
#   This file (backend)     infrastructure/terraform/variables.tf   frontend/.env
#   ---------------------   ------------------------------------    ------------------------------
#   PROJECT_ID              project_id                              REACT_APP_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
#   ALLOWED_ORIGINS         allowed_origins                         -
#   csp_report_only         csp_report_only                         -
#   -                       api_origin                              origin of REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL
#   -                       signer_service_account                  -
#   -                       domain_name                             -
#
# The last three rows have no backend key, and each for its own reason.
#   api_origin is consumed only where a policy is rendered for a browser, and the API's own
#   policy needs no such input because on its own origin 'self' already admits it. See the
#   note at the end of Section B.
#   signer_service_account names the ONE service account Terraform creates. The API runs as
#   that account and signs object URLs as itself, reading the address back out of its own
#   attached credentials, so there is nothing to configure here and nothing to keep in step.
#   A backend setting for it existed and was withdrawn: it could only ever have named the
#   account the runtime already was.
#   domain_name is not independent - the managed certificate serves it, so Terraform refuses
#   to plan unless it is the host of an entry in allowed_origins. Keep ALLOWED_ORIGINS equal
#   to the Terraform allowed_origins list and that check is satisfied automatically.
#
# ONE ORIGIN GRAMMAR
# ------------------
# config.py and variables.tf accept exactly the same origins, character for character: the
# three regular expressions in ORIGIN_PATTERN, HTTPS_ORIGIN_PATTERN and
# LOOPBACK_HTTP_ORIGIN_PATTERN are the same expressions the allowed_origins and api_origin
# validations use. That is asserted by
# backend/tests/test_security.py::TestConfigurationContract - one test compares the
# expressions themselves, another runs 23 origins past both planes and requires the same
# verdict - so the two cannot drift into a state where a value passes one and fails the other.
#
# An origin is exactly scheme://host[:port] and nothing else:
#   * https, or http for localhost, 127.0.0.1 and [::1] only
#   * a lower-case ASCII host whose every dot-separated label starts and ends alphanumeric
#   * an optional port between 1 and 65535
# Rejected, on both planes: a trailing slash or any path, a query string, a fragment, a
# wildcard, embedded credentials, an upper-case or non-ASCII host, an underscore, an empty or
# hyphen-edged label, a trailing dot, and any surrounding whitespace. Whitespace is not
# trimmed on either plane - " https://app.example.com" is a rejection, not a value.
# Database TLS modes are shared in the same spirit: neither plane can express a mode that
# negotiates plaintext.

# =============================================================================
# SECTION A - Core settings (REQUIRED, no defaults)
# =============================================================================
# `Settings` declares exactly SEVENTEEN fields: the six here, which have no defaults, and the
# eleven in Section B, which all do. A missing Section A key raises a Pydantic
# ValidationError at import and the application does not start. Sections A and B together are
# the whole of `Settings.__fields__`, and
# backend/tests/test_security.py::TestConfigurationContract asserts that set exactly - both
# that the count is seventeen and that no eighteenth name has appeared - so this file cannot
# quietly document a key the application does not read, or omit one it does.

# The Google Cloud project ID. Used to build the Secret Manager resource path.
PROJECT_ID=your-gcp-project-id

# SQLAlchemy connection URL for Cloud SQL PostgreSQL 13.
# REQUIRED FOR VALIDATION EVEN THOUGH IT IS OVERWRITTEN: `Settings.__init__` calls
# `super().__init__()` first, so Pydantic validates this key BEFORE the value is
# replaced by the DATABASE_URL secret from Secret Manager. Omitting it fails startup.
#
# GRAMMAR - both this value and the secret that replaces it must satisfy it, and both
# are checked (the environment value by a field validator, the fetched secret where it
# is assigned; `database.py` then refuses to build the engine on any other dialect):
#
#   DRIVER://LOGIN:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE[?sslmode=require]
#
#   DRIVER    postgresql or postgresql+psycopg2, and nothing else. The engine passes
#             connect_args={"sslmode": ...}, which is a psycopg2 argument: asyncpg
#             rejects it and another driver ignores it, either way discarding the
#             transport control. A rejection never quotes the URL, because it carries
#             the password.
#   HOST      In production, the loopback address the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy listens on
#             inside the pod - 127.0.0.1, localhost or [::1]. A private instance IP
#             bypasses the proxy. scripts/deploy.sh refuses the secret otherwise.
#   DATABASE  main-database - the canonical name, equal to the Terraform db_name
#             default and to the database Terraform creates. deploy.sh compares them.
#   LOGIN     A PostgreSQL login you create by hand: Terraform manages no
#             google_sql_user. `gcloud sql users create LOGIN --instance=INSTANCE
#             --password=PASSWORD`, then grant CONNECT on the database plus USAGE and
#             SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on its schema.
#
# The tables are also yours to create. There is no migration tool, no committed DDL
# and no init_db, so until they exist every query fails - authentication included,
# after the token has verified.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+psycopg2://LOGIN:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:5432/main-database

# Celery broker and result backend.
# Same overwrite caveat as DATABASE_URL: validated first, then replaced by the
# REDIS_URL secret. It must be present.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# HMAC signing key for the retained `create_access_token` helper. The live
# authentication path verifies Firebase ID tokens and does not use this key.
# Same overwrite caveat as DATABASE_URL: validated first, then replaced by the
# SECRET_KEY secret. It must be present.
SECRET_KEY=replace-me-with-a-long-random-string

# KEEP. JWT signing algorithm for `create_access_token`. Not overwritten.
ALGORITHM=HS256

# Lifetime in minutes of a token minted by `create_access_token`. Not overwritten.
# Range 1..1440; 1440 minutes is twenty-four hours. A value outside the range is rejected
# at construction. An explicit `expires_delta` argument passed to `create_access_token`
# takes precedence over this setting.
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=15

# =============================================================================
# SECTION B - Security settings (OPTIONAL, each has a default in config.py)
# =============================================================================

# SECURITY: the explicit CORS origin allow-list. Never set this to ["*"]; a wildcard is
# rejected at construction.
# MUST BE A JSON ARRAY. Pydantic v1 parses a List[str] field from the environment
# as JSON, so a bare comma-separated string fails with a parse error. Include
# every origin the browser uses: local development, staging and production.
# The example is a working value for a local `npm start`, and it is deliberately the narrowest
# one - add the deployment's own origins rather than widening it.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=["http://localhost:3000"]

# REPLACE. Cloud Storage bucket holding user uploads. Terraform names it
# `${project_id}-user-uploads`.
# The key is lower-case: `backend/app/services/file_storage.py` reads
# `get_settings().gcs_bucket_name` with this exact spelling.
gcs_bucket_name=your-gcp-project-id-user-uploads

# SECURITY: bounds every object-access grant. Lifetime in minutes of each generated
# V4 signed URL.
# Range 1..10080. 10080 minutes is seven days, the longest lifetime the Cloud
# Storage V4 signing scheme accepts; a value outside the range is rejected at
# construction rather than at the first signing attempt.
signed_url_expiry_minutes=15

# NOT A BACKEND SETTING: signer_service_account
# There is no key for the signing identity, because the application does not need one. The
# API runs as the single service account Terraform creates and signs object URLs as ITSELF,
# reading its own address out of the credentials attached to its runtime; a setting could
# therefore only ever have named the account it already was. It is configured once, as the
# signer_service_account Terraform variable, and reaches the pods through the Workload
# Identity binding in Section D item 2. A backend setting for it existed and was withdrawn.

# SECURITY: database transport mode - the connection previously had no TLS
# setting. Values: disable | require, and nothing else.
# psycopg2's `allow` and `prefer` are REJECTED at construction, because each
# negotiates plaintext whenever the server offers it, silently and with no error to
# observe. `verify-ca` and `verify-full` are rejected too - the reason is at the end of
# this note, because it is a property of how the pods reach the instance.
#
# WHICH VALUE TO USE DEPENDS ON HOW THE PROCESS REACHES THE DATABASE, and the two
# deployments in this repository differ:
#
#   ON GKE, use `disable`. The pods reach Cloud SQL through the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy
#   sidecar, which presents a PLAIN TCP listener on the loopback interface and
#   encrypts its own leg to the instance. That listener offers no TLS and carries no
#   certificate, so a client asking for TLS on it cannot connect at all and `require`
#   fails the handshake outright. Encryption is not lost - the proxy's
#   session to the instance is mutually authenticated, which is what satisfies the
#   instance's ENCRYPTED_ONLY ssl_mode - and the unencrypted leg is a socket that
#   never leaves the pod.
#
#   CONNECTING DIRECTLY to a Cloud SQL address, use `require`. That encrypts the channel
#   but does not verify the server's identity. Verifying it would take `verify-full` with
#   the instance's DNS name and its CA distributed to the client - which this setting does
#   not accept and this infrastructure does not provision. It is a recorded residual.
#
# `disable` is NOT freely selectable. Settings refuses it unless the RESOLVED
# DATABASE_URL - the value read from Secret Manager, not the one in the environment -
# names a local endpoint: host 127.0.0.1, localhost or [::1], or no network host at
# all. A remote host with `disable` fails start-up rather than connecting in
# cleartext. scripts/deploy.sh asserts the same pair before it publishes anything.
#
# Two modes are ACCEPTED here, `require` and `disable`, and exactly one of them is
# DEPLOYABLE on any given topology: `require` for a direct connection, `disable` for the
# proxy sidecar. `verify-ca` and `verify-full` are not accepted at all, because the
# proxy's local listener carries no certificate to validate and a direct connection
# would need the instance CA distributed to every pod. `allow` and `prefer` are not
# accepted either, because each negotiates plaintext silently whenever the server
# offers it, which is the failure this setting exists to prevent.
#
# The default is `require`, so an unset value is never the plaintext mode.
db_sslmode=require

# KEEP. SECURITY: selects the server-side token verification path - client-asserted
# identity was never verified. Values: firebase | legacy_jwt, and nothing else.
# Any other value is REJECTED at construction. Previously any string was accepted and
# anything other than the exact spelling `legacy_jwt` silently took the Firebase path,
# so a misspelled rollback locked legacy callers out while appearing to be applied.
# Read per request, so a change takes effect on the next request with no restart.
#
# `legacy_jwt` IS NOT A ROLLBACK FOR THIS APPLICATION'S BROWSER CLIENTS, and switching to it
# to recover from a lockout makes the lockout total. Its caller contract is a token this
# product has no way to mint: an HS256 JWT signed with SECRET_KEY whose `sub` claim is a
# LOCAL `users.id` integer. Nothing in the repository issues one - no route calls
# `create_access_token`, and the browser holds a Firebase ID token, which is RS256, signed by
# Google, and carries a Firebase UID rather than a local row id. Selecting this mode therefore
# rejects every request the single-page application makes.
# It exists for a caller that mints its own token with SECRET_KEY - a server-to-server client
# or an operator's script - and for that caller it is a working verifier.
# The remedy for a lockout caused by the client failing to attach a token is to revert the
# frontend interceptor, which restores the previous behaviour without reopening the API.
# The break-glass switch below is the other lever, and its cost is stated there.
auth_token_verifier=firebase

# KEEP - the default is the secure position and changing it removes authentication entirely.
# SECURITY: authentication enforcement switch - previously no route required a
# valid token. BREAK-GLASS ONLY. When false, only signature, expiry and revocation
# verification is skipped: the request must still carry an Authorization: Bearer
# header, and it is then admitted on the token's UNVERIFIED claims, which must still
# name a user that exists in the local database. A request carrying no credential is
# refused with 401 {"detail": "Not authenticated"} by the OAuth2 scheme before this
# code runs, whatever this switch is set to - no endpoint is reachable without a
# token. Each bypass is logged and marked on the response with
# X-Auth-Enforcement-Bypassed. Leave this true in every environment.
# NOTE ON THE CURRENT TREE: admission itself cannot complete today. `User` has no
# `workbooks` relationship to match the `back_populates` that `Workbook.owner` declares,
# so SQLAlchemy refuses to configure its mappers and every admitted request - bypassed or
# verified - ends in a logged HTTP 500 instead of reaching a handler. That defect belongs
# to a reference-only module this change set may not edit; it is recorded as a residual and
# a follow-up in documentation/Security Decision Log.md. Rejection paths are unaffected.
auth_enforcement_enabled=true

# THROTTLING - three keys, and two facts about them that are not configurable
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THE WINDOWS ARE COUNTED is not a key. The store is derived from REDIS_URL in
# Section A, so one Redis serves both the Celery broker and the throttling windows and there
# is no second URL to keep in step. A per-process store would let a client's effective quota
# multiply by the number of uvicorn workers and pods serving the API, so the configured
# ceiling would not be the ceiling clients actually got.
#
# WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THAT STORE FAILS is not a key either, and it is worth stating in full
# because the honest answer used to be worse than the documented one. Both tiers keep
# ENFORCING. On the first store error - at start-up or on any later request - the tier logs
# the failure once with its traceback, substitutes a process-local counter, and RE-COUNTS the
# same request against it, so the request that triggered the failure is metered rather than
# waved through. The degraded state persists for the process; `degraded` is exposed on each
# tier for a health endpoint to surface. The cost is precision, not enforcement: counters are
# then per-process, so a client's real ceiling is the configured value times the number of
# processes. It is never unmetered, and no request is admitted because the store was
# unreachable.
#
# WHO IS COUNTED is the socket peer address, always. No forwarded header is read, so a
# caller cannot name the identity its quota is charged to. Behind a proxy that means every
# client behind it is metered as one, which is a real and recorded residual: the ceilings
# below are sized for that, and the alternative - trusting X-Forwarded-For - lets any caller
# that reaches the API directly claim an arbitrary identity.

# KEEP - the default is the secure position; false removes both tiers.
# SECURITY: master switch for both throttling tiers - no route was rate limited.
rate_limit_enabled=true

# KEEP. SECURITY: application-wide per-client request ceiling - request volume was unbounded.
# A window expression such as 600/minute. Counted against ONE bucket per client for the whole
# application, not per route: every request to every URL, including paths that match no route
# at all, decrements the same allowance. Must be generous, because the cell-update endpoint
# fires on every edit and is covered by this ceiling too.
rate_limit_default=600/minute

# SECURITY: tighter ceiling for POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE - write volume was
# unbounded.
# ONE budget is shared by every write a client makes, so this is an AGGREGATE and
# must clear what a normal editing session produces in total, not what one route
# produces. The arithmetic: `frontend/src/services/api.ts` coalesces cell writes into
# at most one request per worksheet per CELL_WRITE_COALESCE_MS (1000 ms), which is 60
# a minute per edited worksheet. 300 admits five worksheets edited at that rate at the
# same time, and four leave 60 a minute for the workbook-creation and share routes.
# Raise it in step with that window if it is shortened, and keep it below
# rate_limit_default so the write tier stays the tighter of the two. Validate against a
# real cell-editing and autosave session before promoting a change.
rate_limit_write=300/minute

# KEEP. SECURITY: Content-Security-Policy enforcement mode - no CSP was emitted on any
# response. When true the policy is sent as Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only,
# which reports violations without blocking. Read when middleware is constructed,
# so a change needs a process or pod restart.
# NOTE: this reaches the three HEADER delivery points only. The <meta> element in
# frontend/public/index.html has no report-only form, so its policy is always
# enforced; it is deliberately the conservative subset that is safe to enforce
# from the first deployment.
csp_report_only=false

# REPLACE, or leave empty. SECURITY: restricts token verification to a single Firebase project
# - the issuer was unconstrained. The Firebase / Identity Platform project ID.
# Leave empty to pin verification to PROJECT_ID from Section A:
# `backend/app/core/security.py` resolves `firebase_project_id or PROJECT_ID` and
# always passes the result to the Admin SDK as an explicit `projectId` option, so
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT is NOT consulted for this setting. Set it only when the
# Firebase project differs from PROJECT_ID.
firebase_project_id=your-firebase-project-id

# NOT A BACKEND SETTING: api_origin
# The API origin admitted to the Content-Security-Policy connect-src allow-list is
# configured on the STATIC delivery paths, not here. The backend emits its own policy on
# API responses, where connect-src governs nothing, so it never reads this value:
#   - infrastructure/terraform/variables.tf  api_origin        (load-balancer header)
#   - infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile.frontend  CSP_CONNECT_SRC_API (container header)
# Both must equal the ORIGIN - scheme://host[:port] and nothing more - of the frontend's
# REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL in Section E, and the container value carries a single leading
# space because the nginx template appends it directly to the source before it.
# scripts/deploy.sh reads the policy the load balancer actually serves and refuses to
# publish a bundle whose API origin it does not admit.

# =============================================================================
# SECTION C - Google Cloud client library variables (NOT `Settings` fields)
# =============================================================================
# These are read directly from the process environment by the Google Cloud client
# libraries and the Firebase Admin SDK. They are not declared on `Settings` and
# never appear in `Settings.__fields__`.

# How the Firebase Admin SDK and the Google Cloud clients resolve the project when
# no explicit option and no service-account key are supplied. Set automatically on
# Google infrastructure such as GKE; set it manually for local development.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=your-gcp-project-id

# Path to a local service-account key file, for Application Default Credentials
# during LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY. The deployed container needs no key file: it uses
# the runtime's attached identity.
# SECURITY REQUIREMENT, not enforced by any build step: keep this file outside every
# image build context. `infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile.backend` runs `COPY . .`
# from the repository root and no `.dockerignore` exists, so a key file placed
# inside the repository IS copied into the image.
GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/local-service-account-key.json

# =============================================================================
# SECTION D - Operator provisioning checklist
# =============================================================================
# These are operator actions, not values to fill in. Each one is a deployment blocker, and
# together with the inputs listed at the end of this section they are the complete set - a
# deployment that satisfies all of it still stops on the four defects listed under KNOWN
# BLOCKERS at the top of scripts/deploy.sh, which live in files outside this change set.
#
# 1. Create the three Secret Manager secrets DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL and
#    SECRET_KEY in project PROJECT_ID, each with a `latest` version. This
#    repository creates no secrets. The application reads all three on every
#    `Settings()` construction and fails if any is absent.
#
# 2. Make the pods authenticate as the service account
#    `infrastructure/terraform/main.tf` creates
#    (google_service_account.url_signer, named by the signer_service_account
#    variable). That configuration creates the account and grants it every role the
#    runtime needs - roles/iam.serviceAccountTokenCreator on itself, object admin on
#    the uploads bucket, cloudsql.client, secretAccessor on the three secrets and
#    datastore.user - but it declares NO Workload Identity binding, so binding the
#    Kubernetes service account to it is an operator step, and the manifests that
#    require it are not in this repository. Do this BEFORE deploying the signed-URL
#    code path: without the account attached, signed-URL generation fails under
#    Application Default Credentials with a missing-private-key error. Also enable
#    the IAM Service Account Credentials API in the project. The backend's
#    signer_service_account setting must equal the Terraform variable of that name.
#
# 3. Keep ALLOWED_ORIGINS consistent with the Identity Platform authorized-domains
#    list in `infrastructure/terraform/main.tf`. If sign-in and CORS disagree about
#    which origins are legitimate, sign-in breaks. Terraform derives that list from
#    allowed_origins by parsing the host out of each entry, so keeping this file's
#    ALLOWED_ORIGINS equal to the Terraform allowed_origins list satisfies it.
#
# 4. Point the DNS A record for domain_name at the reserved global address, then wait
#    for the managed SSL certificate to report ACTIVE before running
#    `scripts/deploy.sh`. Managed certificates require DNS validation and commonly
#    take up to an hour. The whole edge - including the port-80 redirect listener -
#    is created in a SINGLE Terraform apply; there is no two-stage cutover and no
#    flag to flip. The deploy script refuses to publish anything until
#    `gcloud compute ssl-certificates describe` reports ACTIVE and both forwarding
#    rules answer on that address.
#
# 5. Create the database login named in the DATABASE_URL secret. Terraform does not
#    manage it, deliberately: managing the password there would persist it in
#    Terraform state.
#      gcloud sql users create USER --instance=main-instance --prompt-for-password
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# SECTION E - Frontend build-time variables (a DIFFERENT file, NOT `Settings`)
# =============================================================================
# These belong in `frontend/.env`, not in the backend `.env`. react-scripts inlines
# every REACT_APP_* variable into the compiled bundle at build time, so they are
# public by design and none of them is a secret. They are not `Settings` fields and
# never appear in `Settings.__fields__`.
#
# `frontend/src/services/api.ts` initialises the Firebase SDK from these values and
# throws, naming what is missing, when a required one is absent - so the browser
# cannot obtain an ID token and every API call fails.
#
# The clean-machine procedure for this file, including the local development values,
# is in documentation/Developer Onboarding.md under "Configure the request seam".

# SECURITY: base URL the browser calls the API at. TWO constraints are enforced at
# runtime, and a request that breaks either is refused before any token is read:
#   1. it must be an ABSOLUTE http(s) URL, and
#   2. its origin must NOT be the origin serving the application.
# Neither static delivery path routes an API path to the API service, so a relative or
# same-origin value reaches static content and a 200 carrying index.html would be
# returned to callers as workbook data.
# Its ORIGIN - scheme://host[:port] and nothing more - must equal the api_origin
# Terraform variable, and must equal the container's CSP_CONNECT_SRC_API value with the
# single leading space that value carries stripped, or the Content-Security-Policy
# blocks every API call.
REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# Firebase web configuration. The first four are REQUIRED by api.ts.
# REACT_APP_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID must name the same project as the backend
# PROJECT_ID setting: a Firebase ID token names its issuing project in its aud and
# iss claims, so a token from another project is rejected.
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_API_KEY=your-firebase-web-api-key
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=your-gcp-project-id.firebaseapp.com
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=your-gcp-project-id
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:000000000000:web:0000000000000000000000

# Optional: the Auth SDK does not need either, and both are passed through as
# supplied.
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=your-gcp-project-id.appspot.com
REACT_APP_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=000000000000
# =============================================================================
